/bin/zsh -lc "task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260715-1y04r0, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-1y04r0, title=\"Shared tunnel runtime and extension-owned orchestration\", description=\"Build the production ReluxTunnelCore orchestration layer that composes the M0-selected packet bridge, HEV/lwIP integration, and SSH transport into one extension-owned TCP and DNS runtime. Define versioned app-to-extension contracts, deterministic startup and teardown, capability snapshots, and privacy-safe diagnostics without moving live forwarding state into the containing app.\", scope=\"In scope: shared Swift runtime composition, dependency injection, versioned non-secret configuration and control messages, baseline state ownership, deterministic cancellation and cleanup, aggregate metrics, harness integration, migration isolation, and seams reserved for M2 UDP and M3 resilience. Out of scope: re-proving M0 packet or SSH gates, profile editing UI, UDP relay, multi-lane scheduling, path reconnect, fail-closed route mode, release packaging, and changes to the legacy SOCKS product.\", ac=\"1. ReluxTunnelCore owns the live M1 packet, SSH, TCP, DNS, routing, lifecycle, and diagnostics objects after provider start. 2. The containing app can supply only a versioned non-secret snapshot and commands, and can obtain a versioned capability and diagnostic snapshot without being required for forwarding. 3. Startup, cancellation, stop, and partial-failure paths release tasks, descriptors, channels, and packet reads deterministically. 4. Automated harness tests cover ordered startup, injected failures at every boundary, repeated start and stop, and UI-process loss. 5. Runtime documentation identifies M0 inputs, M1 ownership, M2 and M3 extension seams, privacy constraints, and legacy migration isolation.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-eto58m, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-eto58m, title=\"System VPN manager and packet-tunnel lifecycle\", description=\"Implement the iOS and macOS host and provider lifecycle needed to install, start, observe, and stop one owned custom VPN configuration while keeping the packet-tunnel extension authoritative and operational when the containing app is suspended or terminated.\", scope=\"In scope: idempotent NETunnelProviderManager ownership, save and reload, permission flow integration, NETunnelProviderSession start and stop, thin iOS and macOS NEPacketTunnelProvider adapters, versioned app messaging, truthful session and capability status, stop reasons, cleanup, repeated lifecycle tests, and physical-device evidence. Out of scope: profile editor UX, final visual design, UDP relay, reconnect and path-change policy, fail-closed routing, TestFlight, notarization, and M0 entitlement probes.\", ac=\"1. Each platform creates or updates only the Relux-owned manager configuration and does not duplicate or overwrite unrelated VPN configurations. 2. The provider delegates live work to ReluxTunnelCore, reports versioned status, and stops promptly with all owned work cancelled. 3. Forwarding continues while the containing app is suspended or terminated, and the system VPN indicator follows NETunnelProviderSession state. 4. Automated tests cover manager persistence, start cancellation, provider errors, stop reasons, stale configuration, and repeated cycles. 5. Physical iPhone and Apple-silicon Mac evidence demonstrates install, permission, connect, app termination, status recovery, disconnect, and clean repeated operation.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-2wjwuf, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-2wjwuf, title=\"SSH profile authentication and host-key enforcement\", description=\"Turn one versioned non-secret SSH profile snapshot and opaque Keychain credential reference into an authenticated in-process SSH session inside the packet-tunnel extension. Enforce canonical profile validation, mandatory pre-authentication host-key policy, approved public-key authentication, actual endpoint capture, and privacy-safe failures.\", scope=\"In scope: extension-readable profile snapshots, opaque Keychain references, least-privilege credential retrieval, approved host identity records, first-use evidence and trust-required outcome, changed-key rejection, Ed25519 and the M0-approved fallback key type, one baseline SSH session, pre-route endpoint resolution and capture, cancellation, redacted diagnostics, and provider-context tests. Out of scope: key import or generation UI, user trust UI, password authentication, agent forwarding, ProxyJump, interactive shell, SFTP, multi-lane scheduling, and SSH-engine selection.\", ac=\"1. Invalid or stale profiles and inaccessible credential references fail before routes or packet forwarding are installed. 2. Raw host-key evidence is evaluated before user authentication, approved fingerprints connect, first use returns an explicit trust-required result, and a changed key fails without retry or destination channels. 3. Private keys and passphrases remain in the Data Protection Keychain access group and never enter App Group data, providerConfiguration, logs, diagnostics, or board artifacts. 4. The selected M0 SSH adapter authenticates with Ed25519 and the approved fallback against supported fixtures while cancellation and failure paths return resources to baseline. 5. Physical provider-context evidence on iPhone and Mac proves one configured profile can verify, authenticate, capture its real IPv4 or IPv6 SSH endpoint, and expose only privacy-safe status.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-2nqxa5, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-2nqxa5, title=\"TCP SOCKS-to-SSH direct forwarding\", description=\"Implement the process-local SSHProxyAdapter that accepts HEV SOCKS CONNECT flows and maps each accepted flow to one bounded SSH direct-tcpip channel. Preserve independent flow semantics, backpressure, EOF and half-close, cancellation, error mapping, resource limits, and aggregate observability.\", scope=\"In scope: private SOCKS endpoint admission, SOCKS5 CONNECT for IPv4, IPv6, and domain destinations, originator metadata, direct-tcpip channel opens, bounded full-duplex pumping, half-close and reset, cancellation, admission limits, per-flow aggregate metrics, parser fuzzing, conformance tests, end-to-end HEV integration, concurrency, and large-transfer validation. Out of scope: general public proxy exposure, SOCKS authentication UI, UDP ASSOCIATE forwarding, relay framing, lane pools, flow migration, QUIC policy, SSH-engine selection, and application-specific routing.\", ac=\"1. Only the owned HEV and adapter path can reach the internal SOCKS service, and unsupported commands or malformed requests fail within bounded memory. 2. Every accepted CONNECT opens one direct-tcpip channel with the requested destination and remains pinned to the baseline SSH session until close. 3. Bidirectional data, bounded write pressure, EOF, half-close, reset, cancellation, and channel rejection produce deterministic SOCKS or TCP-visible outcomes without leaked tasks or channels. 4. Unit, fuzz, fake-transport, and end-to-end tests cover IPv4, IPv6, domain targets, concurrency, early close, pressure, and repeated cleanup. 5. Representative and multi-gigabyte TCP transfers through the integrated M1 path preserve byte integrity across selected-engine rekey behavior and record throughput, memory, queue, and error evidence.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-2bfjhn, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-2bfjhn, title=\"Leak-free DNS and compatible IPv4/IPv6 routing\", description=\"Install the baseline compatible-mode IPv4 and IPv6 tunnel routes and a tunnel-owned DNS service whose client-facing UDP and TCP requests always leave through SSH as DNS-over-TCP. Resolve and connect the SSH host before default routes, exclude only its actual endpoint, prevent route loops, and prove that ordinary DNS never falls back to the physical resolver.\", scope=\"In scope: baseline resolver-policy decision, virtual address and route contract, pre-route SSH bootstrap resolution, actual endpoint capture, narrow host exclusions, compatible-mode network settings, local UDP and TCP DNS service, tunneled DNS-over-TCP, truncation fallback, bounded positive and negative caching, startup ordering, route and leak tests, external-IP checks, physical-device evidence, and operational documentation. Out of scope: general UDP forwarding, remote relay, DoH unless the resolver decision requires it, fake DNS, fail-closed includeAllNetworks mode, path migration, captive recovery, NAT64 transition resilience, QUIC policy, and M3 reconnect behavior.\", ac=\"1. The SSH connection is established and its actual IPv4, IPv6, or synthesized endpoint is captured before default tunnel routes are applied, and only the required endpoint host exclusion is created. 2. IPv4 and IPv6 application TCP uses the tunnel in compatible mode without routing the SSH transport into itself. 3. The installed DNS configuration points only to the tunnel-owned service, and UDP or TCP client queries are forwarded through SSH using the approved exit-side resolver policy with correct TCP fallback and bounded caching. 4. Resolver or tunnel failure never sends ordinary DNS to a physical resolver and instead returns a bounded explicit failure or stops safe forwarding. 5. Repeatable iPhone and Mac captures show the external IP and DNS observation follow the selected exit host, no ordinary DNS leak or route loop occurs, and documented Apple system exceptions are reported accurately.\")'" in /Users/iv/Developer/relux-proxy
